12	{"type":"finding","severity":"minor","fileName":"rs/crates/libs/paigasus-gateway-core/tests/support/oracle.rs","title":"Reject invalid numeric tokens at end of input instead of dropping them.","comment":"**Reject invalid numeric tokens at end of input instead of dropping them.**

The end-of-input arm in `Partial::value` at `oracle.rs:1122` drops any token made only of `-+.0123456789eE`. This accepts tokens that jiter 0.19.0 rejects. For `partial_json(r#"{"n":01"#)`, `01` fails serde parsing, matches this arm, and the member is dropped. The call returns `{}` instead of an error. jiter 0.19.0 raises `InvalidNumber` for `01`, because the byte after the leading `0` is present in the input. The same arm accepts `1e--`, which jiter rejects with `InvalidNumber` at the second `-`.

The design spec at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-14-sma-757-oracle-argument-prefix-design.md:283-284` requires dropping only an unfinished number at end of input, as jiter does. It does not allow `01` or `1e--`. Limit the arm to valid unfinished number prefixes, such as `10.`, `1e`, and `-`. Reject `01` and `1e--`.

In `run_anthropic_python`, `partial_json(buf)?` at line 1196 succeeds with `{}`. The block receives that value as its `input`, and the run records no error. jiter raises at this event, so the modeled client stops here and records the error. The run snapshot therefore diverges from the modeled client, and any check that compares against that snapshot inherits the divergence. This is a test-oracle fidelity defect. It does not affect production code.

Add rejection cases beside the existing invalid-token assertion:

